1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
Caseworkers write notes full of personal data.
Cloud AI could turn them into emails or handovers in seconds —

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
but pasting a note sends a client's name and diagnosis to a server.
Boundary splits that job in two.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Here is a note about a client: her name, date of birth, address,
her clinic, panic attacks, a medication, her phone and e-mail.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:42,000
Step one runs only on this device. Gemini Nano, through
Firebase AI Logic in only-on-device mode, finds the personal data.

5
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Simple rules catch phone numbers and e-mails;
the model catches names, places and health details.

6
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Each item shows who found it. The ledger on the right confirms:
this call ran on the device and sent nothing.

7
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:40,000
This is exactly what the cloud will see — placeholders,
each with a short hint Nano wrote on device, like "the client, she".

8
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:55,000
If anything still looks personal, the send button locks.
Right before sending, Nano re-checks the outgoing text on device.

9
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:08,000
Step two runs only in the cloud, where a bigger model
writes a better email. It never saw her name.

10
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:20,000
Back on the page, the real details are put back locally —
no model involved.

11
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:35,000
A prefer-on-device check reads the answer for data the cloud invented.
The ledger shows every call, where it ran, and what left the device.

12
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:45,000
If the on-device model is not available, Boundary stops
instead of falling back to the cloud.
